-- What a program holds of the world's things: a handle, the world's
-- number for a thing of the host's -- an element of a page, a buffer on
-- a GPU, a window.  The world numbers the things its effects' providers
-- hand out in one table for them all, so that what one effect hands out
-- another's can take: a `<canvas>` browser.dom found, drawn on by a GPU's
-- effect.  The thing itself stays in the world; the program has its
-- number, plain data, whatever the backend.
--
-- The world's side, for a provider: the world is given to each factory, `export
-- const X = (world) => ...`, and holds what any of them hands out --
--
--   world.hold(x, free)  the number of `x`, a BigInt: a new one, or the
--                        one it has while it is held (`free`, if given:
--                        what lets it go, called with it)
--   world.get(i)         the thing numbered `i`
--   world.release(i)     the thing let go of: `free(x)`, and its number
--                        is no one's again
--
-- and in C, `heff_hold(x, free)`, `heff_held(i)` and
-- `heff_release(i)`, the same.  A handle is `MkHandle i`, made and taken
-- apart by the provider.  A thing is held until it is released, by
-- whichever provider an operation asks to (one of the effect that made
-- it, say): `free` is how its maker said it is let go of.

-- | A thing of the world's, of the kind `t`: the world's number for it.
-- | (`t` says what the thing is -- browser.dom's `Element` -- and has no
-- | value.)
data Handle (t : Type) = MkHandle Int

-- | Whether two handles are of the same thing.  (A thing has one number
-- | while it is held, whoever hands it out.)
-- | >>> sameHandle (t := Unit) (MkHandle 1) (MkHandle 1)
-- | True
-- | >>> sameHandle (t := Unit) (MkHandle 1) (MkHandle 2)
-- | False
sameHandle : {t : Type} -> Handle t -> Handle t -> Bool
sameHandle (MkHandle i) (MkHandle j) = i == j
